# Getting started

This guide takes you from an empty terminal to a running Howdy app. You will
install the tools Howdy builds on, install the `howdy` command line, and
create your first project.

## Before you begin

Howdy apps are written in [Gleam](https://gleam.run/) and run on the Erlang
virtual machine, so a Howdy project will not build or run without these three
tools installed:

- *Gleam*, the language and its build tool
- *Erlang/OTP*, the runtime Gleam compiles to
- *rebar3*, the Erlang build tool Gleam uses to compile some of Howdy's
  dependencies

The Gleam website has up to date instructions for installing all three on
every platform: [Installing Gleam](https://gleam.run/getting-started/installing/).

You also need *Git*, which Gleam uses to download Howdy. The default SQLite
database needs a C compiler to build, and a PostgreSQL project needs a
PostgreSQL server, which the generated project can run for you with Docker.

Check everything is ready:

```sh
gleam --version
erl -version
rebar3 --version
```

## Install the Howdy CLI

The `howdy` command creates new projects for you. It is a single program
written in Go, and there are two ways to install it.

### With the install script

The quickest way is the install script, which downloads the latest build for
your machine from the
[CLI's releases](https://github.com/howdy-gleam/howdy-cli/releases) and
puts `howdy` on your `PATH`:

```sh
curl -fsSL https://tryhowdy.dev/install.sh | bash
```

### With Go

If you have [Go](https://go.dev/doc/install) 1.25 or newer, you can build
and install the CLI from source instead:

```sh
git clone https://github.com/howdy-gleam/howdy-cli.git
cd howdy-cli
go install .
```

Go installs programs to `$HOME/go/bin`, so make sure that directory is on
your `PATH`.

Either way, check the install worked:

```sh
howdy --help
```

## Create your project

Run `howdy new` with a name for your app:

```sh
howdy new my_app
```

Project names follow Gleam's rules: lowercase letters, digits and
underscores, starting with a letter. You can also give a path such as
`apps/my_app`, and the last segment becomes the project name.

The CLI checks that Gleam is installed, writes the project files, formats
them and downloads the dependencies. The new project comes with:

- a themed home page built with Howdy UI
- hot reload for development
- a database with an example todo list, including a JSON API and a live
  component on the home page
- a test suite

### Choose a database

By default the project uses *SQLite*, which needs no setup: the database is a
file created and migrated the first time the app starts.

Pass `--database postgres` to build the same project on *PostgreSQL* instead:

```sh
howdy new my_app --database postgres
```

The app connects using the `DATABASE_URL` setting, and the project includes a
`docker-compose.yml` that runs PostgreSQL on port 5432 with a development
database and a test database, so you will want
[Docker](https://docs.docker.com/get-started/get-docker/) installed. Copy
`.env.example` to `.env` to point `DATABASE_URL` at a different server.

Don't need a database? Create a page-only project instead:

```sh
howdy new my_app --no-database
```

`--database` and `--no-database` cannot be used together.

## Run it

Move into your new project and start the development server:

```sh
cd my_app
gleam dev
```

If you chose PostgreSQL, start the database first. The app and the tests
both need it running:

```sh
cd my_app
docker compose up -d
gleam dev
```

Open <http://127.0.0.1:8789> and say howdy to your new app. Changes to your
code rebuild and refresh the page automatically, and compile errors show up
in the browser while the last working version keeps serving.

When you want to run the app without the reload watcher, use `gleam run`,
and run the tests with `gleam test`.

The `howdy` command does more than create projects. It can add controllers,
records and migrations to your app too: see
[The howdy command](/docs/cli/overview).
